```
// @@@ If the list is initially not empty, then the returned element is the same as the first element of the list before the method execution.
```

Method removeFirst returns E. So we need to assert that if initially not empty (i.e., \old(!this.isEmpty()) or \old(this.size()>0)), then \result equals \old(this.getFirst()) or \old(this.peekFirst())? The spec says "first element of the list before the method execution." There is a method getFirst() that returns first element, but it throws if empty. Since we are in precondition not empty, we can use this.getFirst() before removal. Use \old(this.getFirst()).

Thus assert: \old(!this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()))? But result is of type E, may be null. Equality: need .equals? In earlier examples they used .equals for objects. Use \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst())) but careful if result could be null. Could use (\result == null ? \old(this.getFirst()) == null : \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()))). Simpler: \result == \old(this.getFirst())? For objects, reference equality may not hold. Use .equals is typical. In spec examples they used .equals for query results. So we can assert:

\old(!this.isEmpty()) => \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()))

But need parentheses: (\old(!this.isEmpty()))? Actually \old(!this.isEmpty()) is not correct; \old applies to expression, so we need \old(this.isEmpty())? We want old size >0: \old(this.size()) > 0. So: (\old(this.size()) > 0) => \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst())).

Thus final code:

<code>assert (\old(this.size()) > 0) => \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()));</code>

